{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793373949,"nonce":"SNpJ9U/VoFjJ1DFkDMSn9a1I1ko=","title":"Cloud Sync gateway API: reserve, upload chunks, publish a generation","message":"The Rust Worker (web/cloud) side of Cloud Sync, using native/sync for validation. Routes under an authenticated RayDE account, each checking policy, ACL version, lease, expiry and quota on every call:\n- Create a sync workspace: random workspace id, owner, source type \"git-working-tree\", allocation and expiry from the entitlement, stored in the workspace Durable Object (SQLite) with the first generation 0.\n- Begin a sync: takes the manifest's declared size and unique-chunk list, reserves quota (workspace and account, including in-flight reservations), returns a Publish lease bound to the current ACL version and generation, and the chunks the owner does not already hold (dedup scoped to the workspace or billing owner, with no cross-user existence oracle).\n- Upload a chunk: streamed to private R2 under the workspace prefix, length and SHA-256 checked while streaming, bounded size and rate, resumable (a chunk is idempotent by hash), counted against the reservation.\n- Publish: validate the manifest with Manifest::validate and the lease; check every chunk and the history bundle are durable in R2; compare and swap against the base generation; write the manifest object, advance the generation and start the expiry clock on the first publication, release the reservation into referenced bytes; return the workspace URL only after this. A failed or cancelled upload never replaces the last valid generation. Abandoned uploads are garbage-collected by an alarm and return their reservation.\n- Status, cancel, and export of the current generation.\nVerify with the existing Node fixtures against local R2 emulation: oversized and malformed manifests, a forged checksum, a chunk with the wrong hash, a stale base generation, an expired or revoked lease, quota exhaustion by two concurrent uploads, resume after a dropped connection, and a Durable Object restart between upload and publish. Depends on the account directory and desktop sign-in (92e2f4e). No production deployment is implied.","files":null}]}